Modern feeds are ranked, not purely chronological. The ranking service scores each candidate post before returning the feed.

**Ranking signals:**
- **Engagement probability** — ML model predicting P(like), P(retweet), P(reply) based on user-post affinity
- **Recency** — exponential decay: a 1-hour-old post scores higher than a 12-hour-old post, all else equal
- **Social closeness** — posts from users you interact with frequently rank higher
- **Content type diversity** — avoid showing 10 consecutive image posts; mix content types
- **Creator quality** — accounts with high engagement rates get a boost

The ranking pipeline runs in two stages: a **lightweight pre-ranker** filters 800 candidates to ~200 using simple features (recency, interaction history), then a **full ranker** scores the 200 candidates with the ML model and returns the top 50. This two-stage approach keeps latency under 50ms for the ranking step.

```java
@Service
public class FeedRankingService {
    private static final double RECENCY_DECAY = 0.95; // per hour

    public List<FeedItem> rank(List<FeedItem> candidates, UserProfile user) {
        return candidates.stream()
            .map(item -> {
                double score = 0.0;
                double hoursOld = hoursSince(item.getCreatedAt());
                score += Math.pow(RECENCY_DECAY, hoursOld) * 100;  // recency
                score += interactionScore(user, item.getAuthorId()) * 50;
                score += item.getEngagementRate() * 30;
                item.setRankScore(score);
                return item;
            })
            .sorted(Comparator.comparing(FeedItem::getRankScore).reversed())
            .limit(50)
            .toList();
    }
}
```

For an interview, you don't need to describe the ML model in detail — but showing that you understand **feature engineering** (recency decay, affinity scores, engagement rates) and **the two-stage pipeline** demonstrates maturity beyond "just sort by timestamp."
